      contexto: 'PET-CT shows a tumour\'s metabolic activity and helps determine whether a treatment continues, changes or stops. The National Resources Fund finances it for cancer patients whose general condition allows them to tolerate the specific treatment and with a life expectancy over 3 months, and also in refractory epilepsy and prolonged fever syndrome, with set intervals: 4 weeks after chemotherapy ends, 12 weeks from the last radiotherapy session and 8 weeks from a surgical procedure at the site to be studied. The Fund itself states that "PET is performed at the only existing centre in our country, the Uruguayan Centre for Molecular Imaging (CUDIM)".',
      hallazgo: 'Montevideo Portal reported on 3 February 2026 that "the number of PET-CT scans rose 80%, going from some 4,000 procedures in 2015 to more than 7,000 today", most linked to cancer conditions, and that "cancer scans covered by the National Resources Fund (FNR) are performed at no cost to patients or institutions". IMPO\'s magazine notes that CUDIM performed the country\'s first PET/CT scan in June 2010, that it has accumulated more than 16,000 studies, that close to 70 people work there among physicians, chemists, technicians and administrative and service staff, and that "an agreement between CUDIM and the National Resources Fund (FNR) makes almost all studies free for the patient". In short: the entire national capacity for a publicly funded diagnostic technique is concentrated in a single provider, tied to the Fund by an agreement.',
